# L §6.3 item 2 (round 13): a load conflict is "two rows for one target and day that the read rule doesn't order: at
# the same keys, the later-known one without replace, or tied after precedence (§4.6 steps 2-4)". v1 and v2 are known
# the same day, so neither is later-known; L §4.6 step 3.3 "a row with a span over one with always" orders them, so the
# model loads: before 2026-07-01 only v1's row is a candidate (2), from it v2's span wins (1).
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-12-31
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "s"

input x: int

version v1
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2025-12-01
  x = 2

version v2
  cite s
  governs 2026-07-01..
  known 2025-12-01
  x = 1

ask q1: x law 2026-03-01
ask q2: x law 2026-09-01
=== expect
q1: 2
  law date 2026-03-01, cut 2026-12-31
q2: 1
  law date 2026-09-01, cut 2026-12-31
